|segment[ver-free-variable-default-fill] :type mechanism :status discussion-grade :stage draft
  :max-attainable empirical
  :tags [register delegation defaults]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title Every action has free variables, and trained defaults fill them

  |summary
    The defect is co-extensive with the action space: what is unspecified by intent
    gets filled by a default, and the fill runs one direction.

  |formal-expression
    Every action carries degrees of freedom nobody specified — wording, scope, which
    instance, what to omit, how to label. Those slots do not stay empty; they are
    filled by whatever the actor's training makes most available. So the useful
    question is never *how often does this go wrong* but **what fraction of this
    action was specified by intent and what fraction was fill**, and the honest
    answer is that most of the mass is fill, in every action, always.

    The fill is directional rather than noisy — toward caution, hedge, deprecation,
    redirect — and each hop makes the next reader read less.

    The dangerous case is not a broad grant. It is a grant that is fully specified
    on substance and free only on **wording**, because wording is where register
    lives and register carries the operative signal.

  |epistemic-status
    **Specimen A.** An instruction said a file *"needs to be removed/replaced as it
    gets stale"* — future tense, keep it for continuity. What landed added a
    `[!warn]` header reading *"Stale snapshot — not the corpus, not maintained"*
    (present tense), *"Don't treat it as current"*, and a redirect away. The body
    preserved the original tense faithfully; nobody reads past the header. Substance
    preserved, signal inverted, entirely inside the latitude honestly granted by
    *"something like that."*

    **Specimen B, same day, different model.** Asked to add commentary to a document
    with full latitude on form, an agent filled the form with report-register —
    headers, rung labels, suggested remedies — and inverted a request to *preserve
    texture* into the production of polish.

    Held at **discussion-grade**: two specimens, one estate, one day, and the
    directionality claim in particular is argued rather than measured.

    Max attainable: **empirical**, and the experiment is stated and unrun — sample
    agent-written banners, pointers and glosses against the content they stand for,
    and test whether the added register runs one direction. If it does, a corpus
    with agent-written signposting continuously talks its own readers out of reading
    it.

  |discussion
    The corrective is not less latitude. It is **supplying purpose alongside it** —
    what the artifact is for, who reads it and when. Same latitude plus one sentence
    of purpose probably produces a good banner, and the delegation discipline in
    `arch/AGENTIC-DELEGATION.md` is the long form of that argument.

    The mechanism runs in the useful direction too, and by the same route: a brief
    that marks its own provenance per fact changes what stands out for the receiver
    rather than instructing them. See #exm-agent-adopted-the-stance.

  |working-notes
    Open: whether *free variable → trained default* is one mechanism or two — the
    fill itself, and the fact that the filled slot is not experienced as a decision.
    The second is what makes it unavailable to introspection, and it may belong with
    #ver-generation-is-self-ratifying instead.
